/* ===== "It really happened": one real case per module, placed in the section it illustrates =====
   Each story is a 'fig' block, so adding it never shifts saved progress keys. Facts were checked
   against regulator, court and company sources in October 2026; see the sources listed on each card. */
(function(){
'use strict';
const S=[
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 1 */
{m:'m1',s:'cf',co:'W.T. Grant',where:'United States',year:'1975',
 title:'Profitable on paper, broke in the bank',
 stat:{n:10,f:'# years',l:'how early its cash flow showed the problem, according to a 1980 study'},
 body:[`In the early 1970s W.T. Grant was one of America's biggest store chains, with more than 1,000 shops. To lift sales it gave customers easy credit. Sales grew, reported profits held up until 1973, and the dividend stayed at $1.50 a share.`,
  `But the cash wasn't arriving. It was sitting in customers' unpaid accounts (receivables reached $602 million) and in stock on the shelves. Grant filled the gap by borrowing, until it owed about $600 million to 143 banks. In the year to January 1975 it lost $177 million, and in October 1975 it filed for bankruptcy: the largest retail failure in US history at the time.`],
 think:`For years Grant's income statement looked fine. Which statement would have warned you, and what would it have shown?`,
 reveal:[`The cash flow statement. A 1980 study of Grant's accounts by Largay and Stickney found that its operations had been a net <em>user</em> of cash: running the business produced almost no cash for a decade, while profit and the "funds from operations" measure analysts watched at the time looked stable. Cash flow analysis would have flagged trouble about ten years before the collapse; the usual ratios only did so around 1970–71.`],
 lesson:`Read the three pictures together. Profit that never turns into cash, while receivables and borrowing climb, is the pattern to stop early. It's BraceWorks' "best month ever" case, at the scale of a thousand stores.`,
 src:[['Largay & Stickney, Financial Analysts Journal (1980)','https://rpc.cfainstitute.org/research/financial-analysts-journal/1980/cash-flows-ratio-analysis-and-the-wt-grant-company-bankruptcy'],['W.T. Grant annual reports (archive.org)','https://archive.org/details/wtgrantcoannualreports']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 2 */
{m:'m2',s:'close',co:'Parmalat',where:'Italy',year:'2003',
 title:'The €3.95 billion bank account that didn\'t exist',
 stat:{n:3.95,d:2,f:'€#bn',l:'of "cash" at Bank of America that was never there'},
 body:[`Parmalat was a family-controlled Italian dairy group with about 36,000 employees. Its accounts said a Cayman Islands subsidiary, Bonlat, held €3.95 billion in cash and securities at Bank of America in New York, and the auditors had a letter from the bank confirming it.`,
  `In December 2003 Bank of America said the account didn't exist. The confirmation was a forgery: according to evidence reported from the prosecution, it had been made on a company computer that held a copy of the bank's logo, and the computer was later smashed with a hammer. Within a week Parmalat was insolvent with a hole of about €14 billion, then Europe's largest bankruptcy.`],
 think:`The auditors had a bank confirmation in their file. What was wrong with how they got it?`,
 reveal:[`It came through the company, not straight from the bank. The people who kept the books also controlled the "independent" evidence. The US SEC called it one of the largest financial frauds in history. Founder Calisto Tanzi was sentenced to prison twice: 10 years in 2008, then 18 years in 2010.`],
 lesson:`Every month, reconcile each bank account to a statement that comes from the bank itself: read-only online access for you, not a PDF passed on by the bookkeeper. At year-end, bank confirmations should go from the bank directly to the auditor.`,
 src:[['SEC litigation release 18527 (2004)','https://www.sec.gov/litigation/litreleases/lr18527.htm'],['Jurist: Tanzi convicted (2010)','https://www.jurist.org/news/2010/12/italy-court-convicts-former-parmalat-owner-over-fraudulent-bankruptcy/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 3 */
{m:'m3',s:'margins',co:'Groupon',where:'United States',year:'2011',
 title:'The profit measure that left out marketing',
 stat:{n:420,f:'$#m',l:'operating loss in 2010, presented as a $61m "adjusted" profit'},
 body:[`When Groupon filed to list its shares in June 2011, it was growing fast by spending heavily on online marketing to win subscribers. In 2010 it made an operating loss of $420 million. But its filing led with a measure it had invented: "adjusted consolidated segment operating income". It added back $242 million of online marketing, plus share-based pay and acquisition costs, and turned the loss into a $61 million profit.`,
  `The US regulator pushed back: online marketing "appears" to be "a normal, recurring operating cash expenditure", and leaving it out created a measure that was "potentially misleading". Groupon dropped it in August. A month later it also cut its reported 2010 revenue from $713 million to $313 million, after switching from counting the full price customers paid to counting only its own share.`],
 think:`Marketing was the cost of winning every customer. What one question would have exposed the "adjusted" profit?`,
 reveal:[`"What did you take out, and will it happen again next year?" A cost the business needs every year isn't an adjustment. After listing, Groupon also had to revise its first quarterly results for higher refunds, and its auditor reported a material weakness in its controls.`],
 lesson:`When a pack shows "adjusted", "underlying" or "normalised" profit, ask for the list of items removed and how often each has appeared before. Restructuring that is "one-off" every year is simply a cost of doing business.`,
 src:[['SEC comment letter and Groupon reply (July 2011)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001490281/000104746911006336/filename1.htm'],['CNN Money: Groupon accounting (2011)','https://money.cnn.com/2011/08/10/technology/groupon_accounting/index.htm']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 4 */
{m:'m4',s:'health',co:'Lehman Brothers',where:'United States',year:'2008',
 title:'Tidying the balance sheet for the photo',
 stat:{n:50.4,d:1,f:'$#bn',l:'of debt moved off the balance sheet for the June 2008 quarter-end'},
 body:[`Investors judged Lehman Brothers partly on its leverage: how much it had borrowed for each dollar of equity. Just before each quarter-end in 2007 and 2008 it used a transaction it called "Repo 105". It handed over securities for cash for a few days, booked this as a <em>sale</em> rather than a loan, used the cash to pay down debt, and took the securities back days after the reporting date.`,
  `The sums were huge: $38.6 billion at the end of 2007, $49.1 billion in March 2008 and $50.4 billion in June 2008. At the June quarter-end, reported net leverage was 12.1 times instead of 13.9. The court-appointed examiner later found that Repo 105 moved leverage "not by tenths, but by whole points", without being disclosed. One senior executive had called it, in an email, "another drug we r on". Lehman collapsed in September 2008, the largest bankruptcy in US history.`],
 think:`On the reporting date, Lehman's balance sheet was arithmetically correct. Why did it still mislead?`,
 reveal:[`A balance sheet is one photo on one day, and Lehman staged the photo. The debt disappeared for the snapshot and came back a few days later. Nothing about the business had changed.`],
 lesson:`Compare month-end balances with the average during the month, and ask why cash, debt, payables or stock jump just before or after a reporting date. Delaying supplier payments by a week, or pushing a customer to pay early, can make BraceWorks' ratios look better than they really are.`,
 src:[['Examiner\'s report, volume 3 (2010)','https://web.stanford.edu/~jbulow/lehmandocs/VOLUME%203.pdf'],['Valukas testimony to the US Senate (2011)','https://www.banking.senate.gov/download/040611valukas-testimony']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 5 */
{m:'m5',s:'bridge',co:'Carillion',where:'United Kingdom',year:'2018',
 title:'Paying dividends with suppliers\' money',
 stat:{n:29,f:'£#m',l:'of cash left at the collapse, against liabilities of nearly £7 billion'},
 body:[`Carillion built hospitals, schools and railways and employed about 43,000 people. It reported profits, and paid a record £79 million dividend, £55 million of it in June 2017. Behind that, contract profits were booked optimistically, and suppliers were made to wait 120 days to be paid. Those who wanted their money sooner could get it at 45 days through a bank scheme, for a fee; Carillion repaid the bank later.`,
  `That scheme was borrowing in all but name, about £472 million of it, but it was counted as money owed to suppliers, not as debt. Over 2013–16 Carillion reported £509 million of operating cash flow against £501 million of operating profit. Treat the bank scheme as the borrowing it was, and Moody's estimated cash conversion fell to about 7%.`,
  `A month later Carillion wrote its contracts down by £845 million, later raised to more than £1 billion. It went into liquidation in January 2018. Parliament's report called it "a story of recklessness, hubris and greed", and the auditor KPMG was later fined a record £21 million.`],
 think:`Carillion's operating cash flow matched its profit almost exactly. Why wasn't that reassuring?`,
 reveal:[`Because the cash came from paying suppliers later and quietly borrowing through the bank scheme, not from the contracts. Strip that out and four years of operations had produced about £37 million of cash: almost nothing.`],
 lesson:`When operating cash flow improves, ask which line did it. Rising payable days mean cash is being borrowed from suppliers, and supplier finance is debt in disguise. Never pay a dividend that operating cash hasn't earned.`,
 src:[['UK Parliament joint committee report (2018)','https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201719/cmselect/cmworpen/769/76903.htm'],['National Audit Office investigation (2018)','https://www.nao.org.uk/press-releases/investigation-into-the-governments-handling-of-the-collapse-of-carillion/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 6 */
{m:'m6',s:'build',co:'Boeing 787',where:'United States',year:'2015–2021',
 title:'Averaging the cost of planes not yet built',
 stat:{n:28.5,d:1,f:'$#bn',l:'of 787 build costs carried forward as an asset at the end of 2015'},
 body:[`A new aircraft costs far more to build at the start than later on, as workers learn and suppliers improve. Boeing uses "program accounting": it estimates how many 787s it will sell and their average cost, and charges each delivered plane at that average. When an early plane costs more than the average, the excess stays on the balance sheet as "deferred production costs", to be recovered from cheaper planes later.`,
  `For years Boeing reported a profit on each 787 it delivered while the deferred costs grew to about $28.5 billion by the end of 2015 (more than $32 billion including tooling). One academic case study calculated that without the deferral, Boeing's equity in early 2015 would have been negative.`,
  `Then the assumptions moved. In 2020 Boeing cut the number of 787s in its accounting plan by 100, and the programme's margin fell to near break-even. Quality problems halted deliveries in 2021, and Boeing took a $3.5 billion loss on the 787 that December quarter.`],
 think:`Program accounting is perfectly legal. So where was the risk?`,
 reveal:[`Every reported profit rested on forecasts of volume, unit cost and price years ahead. Optimistic assumptions don't remove a loss; they postpone it, and it lands all at once when the forecast changes.`],
 lesson:`BraceWorks' unit costs also rest on assumptions: the volume behind the overhead rate, and the standard hours per product. Compare actual unit cost with standard every month, and reset the standard as soon as the assumption behind it stops being believable.`,
 src:[['Boeing Q4 2015 results call (SEC filing)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000012927/000001292716000094/a201601jan27exhibit991newt.htm'],['CPA Journal: the effects of program accounting (2016)','https://www.cpajournal.com/2016/11/23/case-study-effects-program-accounting/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 7 */
{m:'m7',s:'case',co:'McKesson & Robbins',where:'United States',year:'1938',
 title:'The warehouses that weren\'t there',
 stat:{n:19,f:'$#m',l:'of stock and customer balances that didn\'t exist'},
 body:[`McKesson & Robbins was a respected US drug wholesaler, run by a president called F. Donald Coster. Its 1937 balance sheet showed $44 million of inventory and $26 million owed by customers, much of it from a trading business in crude drugs.`,
  `In 1938 the treasurer, Julian Thompson, grew suspicious of a $3 million loan request and checked on the trading business's sales agent. Its credit report was forged. About $10 million of the stock and $9 million of the receivables were fiction, backed by fake purchase orders and shipping papers produced by Coster's own brothers under false names. "Coster" himself turned out to be Philip Musica, a fraudster with earlier convictions. The day after fingerprints identified him, on 16 December 1938, he shot himself.`,
  `The auditors never inspected the stock, and at the president's request they never wrote to the customers to confirm what they owed.`],
 think:`What two simple checks would have exposed the fraud?`,
 reveal:[`Counting the stock, and confirming big customer balances with the customers themselves. After the scandal, both became standard audit steps (1939), and they still are.`],
 lesson:`Join a stock count at least once a year. Pick items from the ledger and find them on the shelf, then pick items on the shelf and find them in the ledger. Ask your biggest customers to confirm in writing what they owe.`,
 src:[['SEC Historical Society: 1939 papers on McKesson & Robbins','https://www.sechistorical.org/collection/papers/1930/1939_0304_AIAMcKesson.pdf'],['SEC Historical Society: the profession responds','https://sechistorical.org/museum/galleries/rca/rca02a-profession.php']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 8 */
{m:'m8',s:'split',co:'Kraft Heinz',where:'United States',year:'2015–2019',
 title:'Savings that hadn\'t been earned yet',
 stat:{n:208,f:'$#m',l:'of "cost savings" reversed in the 2019 restatement'},
 body:[`After the 2015 merger of Kraft and Heinz, procurement teams had demanding cost-saving targets, and managers' bonuses depended on hitting them. According to the US SEC, some teams closed the gap by booking supplier deals as savings before they were earned: a supplier paid money up front in return for a longer contract and higher prices later, and the whole payment was recorded at once as lower cost of goods.`,
  `In one example in the SEC's order, a sugar supplier paid a $2 million "prebate" for a three-year extension, offset by higher sugar prices over those same years. All $2 million was booked as a saving immediately. Internal messages quoted by the SEC talk of needing to "jam" a rebate in to help results.`,
  `In February 2019 the company disclosed the investigation, alongside a $15.4 billion write-down of goodwill and brand values, and its shares fell as much as 28% in a day. The restatement reversed $208 million of savings from nearly 300 deals. In 2021 Kraft Heinz paid a $62 million penalty, and two former executives settled charges without admitting or denying them.`],
 think:`Purchasing reports a big favourable price variance. What should you ask before celebrating?`,
 reveal:[`Whether the saving is real and earned yet. Does the contract tie it to future volumes, a longer commitment or price rises later? Money paid for future business belongs to future periods. And when a buyer's bonus depends on reported savings, look at the contracts behind the biggest ones.`],
 lesson:`A favourable price variance is good news only if the price will hold. Ask purchasing to show next year's price in the same contract, and treat up-front rebates as income spread over the life of the deal.`,
 src:[['SEC litigation release 25195 (2021)','https://www.sec.gov/litigation/litreleases/2021/lr25195.htm'],['Harvard Law School Forum summary (2021)','https://corpgov.law.harvard.edu/2021/09/16/sec-charges-kraft-heinz-with-improper-expense-management-scheme/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 9 */
{m:'m9',s:'case',co:'Tesco',where:'United Kingdom',year:'2014',
 title:'The £263 million that belonged to later periods',
 stat:{n:263,f:'£#m',l:'of profit overstated, mostly by booking supplier income early'},
 body:[`In August 2014 Tesco, Britain's biggest retailer, told investors to expect about £1.1 billion of trading profit for the half-year. Three weeks later its new chief executive, Dave Lewis, 18 days into the job, was told that an employee had raised concerns. Supplier income (payments from suppliers for promotions and volume rebates) had been booked before it was earned, and some costs had been booked late.`,
  `Tesco announced the problem on 22 September 2014 and its shares fell about 12% that morning. Deloitte's review put the overstatement at £263 million, across the half-year and earlier periods. Eight executives were suspended and the chairman stepped down.`,
  `Tesco paid a £129 million fine under a deferred prosecution agreement with the Serious Fraud Office, and about £85 million in compensation to investors. Three former executives were charged; all three were acquitted.`],
 think:`Management reports went to the top of Tesco every month. Why didn't they show the problem?`,
 reveal:[`Supplier income sat inside the margin, mixed with sales. Nothing showed how much of it was estimated, when it had been earned, or whether suppliers had agreed it in writing. Each period met its target by borrowing income from the next.`],
 lesson:`Ask for rebates and other supplier income on their own line in the monthly pack, split between agreed in writing and estimated. A month that lands exactly on budget thanks to "other income" deserves a question.`,
 src:[['Serious Fraud Office: Tesco plc','https://www.sfo.gov.uk/cases/tesco-plc/'],['FCA: Tesco to pay redress for market abuse (2017)','https://www.fca.org.uk/news/press-releases/tesco-pay-redress-market-abuse']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 10 */
{m:'m10',s:'margin',co:'Kanthal',where:'Sweden',year:'1980s',
 title:'The best customer was the biggest money-loser',
 stat:{n:225,f:'#%',l:'of the profit came from 20% of the customers'},
 body:[`Kanthal, a Swedish maker of heating wire and elements, spread all its selling and administration costs over customers as a flat percentage of sales. On that basis every customer looked about equally profitable.`,
  `In the 1980s it changed the system. Staff were interviewed about what their time actually went on, and costs were charged to each order, with an extra charge for items it didn't normally stock. The result, according to the consultants who did the work (it later became a Harvard teaching case): 20% of customers produced 225% of the profit, and the rest lost money between them. The customer everyone considered most important turned out to be the least profitable of all.`,
  `Kanthal didn't simply drop the loss-makers. It changed how it served them, for example by automating order handling for selected customers, which made them profitable.`],
 think:`Why did a flat overhead percentage hide this for years?`,
 reveal:[`It charged a large, simple order and a small, fiddly one the same cost per dollar of sales. Customers who order little and often, want special items or need lots of support cost far more to serve than their sales suggest.`],
 lesson:`Before deciding which customers to grow or drop, measure the cost to serve each one: number of orders, special items, support time and payment terms. Then fix the price or the process, not just the customer list.`,
 src:[['Harvard Business School case: Kanthal (A)','https://store.hbr.org/product/kanthal-a/190002'],['SAM: Kanthal profitability analysis','https://sam.se/cases/kanthal-profitability-analysis/']]},
{m:'m10',s:'cash13',co:'Patisserie Valerie',where:'United Kingdom',year:'2018',
 title:'Millions in the bank, or so the board thought',
 stat:{n:28.8,d:1,f:'£#m',l:'of cash reported in the May 2018 half-year results'},
 body:[`Patisserie Valerie was a fast-growing UK café chain with about 200 sites. In May 2018 its half-year results reported about £28.8 million of cash. In October trading in its shares was suspended over "potentially fraudulent" accounting. The company had two overdrafts, with HSBC and Barclays, of almost £10 million, which its chairman said neither he, the board nor the auditors had known about. It also emerged that in September the tax authority had petitioned to wind up its main trading subsidiary over an unpaid £1.14 million bill.`,
  `The chairman lent £20 million and a share sale raised £15.7 million, but in January 2019 the business went into administration: 70 cafés closed and about 900 jobs went. Estimates of the overstatement range from about £30 million to £94 million. The auditor was fined £2.3 million. Four people, including the former finance director, have been charged by the Serious Fraud Office; they deny the charges, and the trial is listed for 2028.`],
 think:`The board saw a cash figure every month. What was missing?`,
 reveal:[`An independent view of the bank. Nobody outside the finance team was checking bank statements or asking the banks for a list of every account and facility in the company's name, so cash that wasn't there, and overdrafts that were, went unnoticed.`],
 lesson:`A 13-week forecast is only as good as its opening cash. Make sure that figure comes from the bank, not the ledger: have read-only online access to every account, and once a year ask each bank to confirm all accounts and borrowing in the company's name.`,
 src:[['Serious Fraud Office: Patisserie Holdings','https://www.gov.uk/sfo-cases/patisserie-holdings-plc'],['Restaurant Online: Grant Thornton fined (2021)','https://www.restaurantonline.co.uk/Article/2021/09/27/Grant-Thornton-hit-with-2.3m-fine-over-Patisserie-Valerie-collapse']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 11 */
{m:'m11',s:'good',co:'Wells Fargo',where:'United States',year:'2016',
 title:'The KPI behind 3.5 million unwanted accounts',
 stat:{n:3.5,d:1,f:'# million',l:'accounts that may have been opened without customers\' permission'},
 body:[`Wells Fargo prided itself on "cross-selling": the number of its products each customer held. The goal was eight per customer. Explaining the number in a 2010 letter to shareholders, the chief executive reportedly wrote that it "rhymed with 'great'". Branch staff had demanding daily sales targets, and their jobs depended on meeting them.`,
  `Many met them by opening accounts customers had never asked for, sometimes moving customers' money into them or forging signatures. In 2016 regulators fined the bank $185 million; it had already fired about 5,300 employees. A wider review found about 3.5 million potentially unauthorised accounts between 2009 and 2016. The chief executive resigned and gave up $41 million of share awards. In 2020 the bank paid $3 billion to settle criminal and civil investigations. An internal investigator had warned of a "growing plague" as early as 2004.`],
 think:`The cross-selling figure kept improving. Why was it the wrong signal?`,
 reveal:[`Once a measure becomes a target, people find the cheapest way to move it (Goodhart's law). Nothing balanced it: no matching measure of accounts actually used, unused accounts closed, or customer complaints.`],
 lesson:`Pair every target with a check on quality: sales with returns and credit notes, output with scrap and rework, on-time delivery with complaints. Be wary of any KPI that improves faster than the business does.`,
 src:[['CFPB: Wells Fargo fined $100 million (2016)','https://www.consumerfinance.gov/about-us/newsroom/consumer-financial-protection-bureau-fines-wells-fargo-100-million-widespread-illegal-practice-secretly-opening-unauthorized-accounts/'],['US Department of Justice: $3 billion settlement (2020)','https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/wells-fargo-agrees-pay-3-billion-resolve-criminal-and-civil-investigations-sales-practices']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 12 */
{m:'m12',s:'be',co:'MoviePass',where:'United States',year:'2017–2019',
 title:'Every new customer made the losses bigger',
 stat:{n:21.7,d:1,f:'$#m',l:'average cash shortfall a month, September 2017 to April 2018'},
 body:[`In August 2017 MoviePass cut its price to $9.95 a month for up to one cinema ticket a day. It paid the cinema the full ticket price, often $10 to $15, every time a member went. Subscribers rocketed from about 400,000 to more than 3 million by June 2018.`,
  `Every member who saw more than one film a month cost more than they paid, so the faster MoviePass grew, the faster it lost money: an average cash shortfall of about $21.7 million a month from September 2017 to April 2018, and about $40 million in May 2018 alone. When a radio host suggested it was losing $21 million a year, the chief executive corrected her: "No, a month."`,
  `The parent company's shares fell about 98%. MoviePass shut down in September 2019, the parent went bankrupt in 2020, and both chief executives later pleaded guilty to securities-fraud charges over what they had told investors.`],
 think:`What does break-even analysis say about a product whose contribution per unit is negative?`,
 reveal:[`There is no break-even point. Fixed costs never get covered, because every unit sold adds to the loss. Volume only helps when each unit contributes something.`],
 lesson:`Before chasing volume with a low price (a big tender, a new distributor, a launch), check the contribution per unit after freight, commission and payment terms. If it's negative, more orders only mean bigger losses.`,
 src:[['Helios & Matheson 8-K filing (May 2018)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001040792/000121390018005649/f8k050818_heliosandmath.htm'],['NPR interview transcript (2018)','https://www.npr.org/transcripts/622697575'],['FTC settlement (2021)','https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2021/06/operators-moviepass-subscription-service-agree-settle-ftc-allegations-they-limited-usage-failed']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 13 */
{m:'m13',s:'make',co:'Boeing 787',where:'United States',year:'2009–2011',
 title:'Outsourced to save money, then had to buy the factory',
 stat:{n:580,f:'$#m',l:'paid to buy a struggling supplier\'s 787 factory'},
 body:[`For the 787, Boeing handed far more of the design and building to outside suppliers than on earlier aircraft. Partners built whole sections and Boeing assembled them. The aim was to share the investment and the risk and to build faster.`,
  `It didn't work out that way. Suppliers struggled with the new technology and with coordinating their work, and Boeing had to step in. In July 2009 it agreed to buy Vought's plant in South Carolina, which built the 787's rear fuselage, for about $580 million in cash. Vought's chief executive said the programme's financial demands were "clearly growing beyond what a company our size can support".`,
  `The first 787 was delivered in September 2011, more than three years late. In January 2011 the head of Boeing's commercial aircraft business said publicly that the outsourcing strategy had backfired: in substance, rescuing it had cost more than keeping key work in-house would have.`],
 think:`On paper, the outsourced parts were cheaper. What did that comparison leave out?`,
 reveal:[`The cost of managing many suppliers, whether they could actually do the work, the time lost when one of them fell behind, and the price of rescuing them.`],
 lesson:`When BraceWorks compares a supplier's quote with its own cost, add what isn't on the quote: quality checks, longer lead times, safety stock, management time, and what happens if the supplier fails or raises prices once you depend on it.`,
 src:[['Boeing: agreement to acquire Vought operations (2009)','https://boeing.mediaroom.com/2009-07-07-Boeing-Announces-Agreement-to-Acquire-Vought-Operations-in-South-Carolina'],['Boeing 2009 annual report (Form 10-K)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000012927/000119312510024406/d10k.htm']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 14 */
{m:'m14',s:'play',co:'Iridium',where:'United States',year:'1998–2000',
 title:'A $5 billion network sold for $25 million',
 stat:{n:5,f:'$#bn',l:'invested in 66 satellites; the whole system later sold for $25 million'},
 body:[`Iridium, backed by Motorola, put 66 satellites into orbit so that one phone could work anywhere on Earth. Service began on 1 November 1998. The handset cost about $3,000, and calls cost $4 to $8 a minute.`,
  `The plan needed about a million customers to break even. By the end of March 1999 Iridium had 10,294. Its bank loans required 52,000 subscribers by then and $30 million of revenue in total; it had $1.6 million, and lost $505 million in that quarter alone.`,
  `Iridium filed for bankruptcy on 13 August 1999 and switched off its service the following March. In December 2000 a group of investors bought the entire system for $25 million: about half a cent for every dollar invested. Rebuilt with a smaller cost base, the new business needed only about 60,000 customers to work.`],
 think:`What test would have shown how fragile the investment case was?`,
 reveal:[`A sensitivity test on volume. The whole case rested on one optimistic demand figure, while cheaper mobile networks were spreading fast. And because the banks wrote the plan into the loan conditions, missing it didn't just lower the return; it triggered default.`],
 lesson:`For every investment, ask what happens if volume is half, or a fifth, of plan, and whether the loan conditions assume the plan. If the answer is "we can't pay the bank", the project is riskier than its NPV says.`,
 src:[['Iridium quarterly report to the SEC (Q1 1999)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000948421/000095013399001884/0000950133-99-001884.txt'],['Smithsonian Air & Space: the rise and fall and rise of Iridium','https://www.smithsonianmag.com/air-space-magazine/the-rise-and-fall-and-rise-of-iridium-5615034/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 15 */
{m:'m15',s:'why',co:'Toshiba',where:'Japan',year:'2008–2015',
 title:'When the budget is an order, not a forecast',
 stat:{n:151.8,d:1,f:'¥#bn',l:'of pre-tax profit overstated over about seven years'},
 body:[`At Toshiba, top management gave divisions profit targets called "challenges". They weren't forecasts of what the business could earn; they were numbers to be delivered. An independent committee found that divisions met them by delaying losses and pushing costs into later periods. In one example, a project's estimated cost of ¥9 billion was replaced with a "challenge" figure of ¥7 billion, and no provision was made for the expected loss.`,
  `The committee's 2015 report found pre-tax profit overstated by ¥151.8 billion between 2008 and 2014, and described a culture in which employees could not act against the wishes of their superiors. The chief executive and his predecessor resigned with other senior executives, regulators imposed a record ¥7.37 billion fine, and in 2016 Toshiba sold its medical equipment business to Canon for ¥665.5 billion to repair its finances.`],
 think:`Why is a budget handed down as an order dangerous?`,
 reveal:[`If a target can't be met honestly, people meet it with accounting: provisions not made, costs delayed, revenue pulled forward. The numbers then tell management what it wants to hear, until they can't any more.`],
 lesson:`Build the budget from volumes, prices and costs, then stretch it; don't start from the answer. Make it safe to say "we won't make it", and watch for provisions and accruals that shrink just as a target is met.`,
 src:[['Fortune: record fine for Toshiba (2015)','https://fortune.com/2015/12/07/japan-watchdog-toshiba-fine'],['Taipei Times: Canon buys Toshiba Medical (2016)','https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/biz/archives/2016/03/18/2003641825']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 16 */
{m:'m16',s:'ccc',co:'Thomas Cook',where:'United Kingdom',year:'2019',
 title:'A 178-year-old company and its winter cash',
 stat:{n:200,f:'£#m',l:'extra winter facility the lenders asked for, which couldn\'t be found'},
 body:[`Holiday companies collect most of their cash in spring and summer, when customers pay for trips, and pay hotels and airlines later. By winter the cash has drained away, so the low point arrives at the same time every year.`,
  `Thomas Cook, founded in 1841, went into 2019 heavily in debt. It lost £1.45 billion in the six months to March 2019, mostly from writing off goodwill from a 2007 merger. A £900 million rescue was agreed in August. Then the lenders asked for a further £200 million standby facility to see the company through the winter low. The money couldn't be found, and on 23 September 2019 Thomas Cook collapsed.`,
  `About 150,000 UK holidaymakers were abroad; the aviation authority flew more than 140,000 of them home on 746 flights. About 21,000 jobs were lost. In 2025 the audit regulator fined the auditor £4.875 million, in part for not challenging management enough on going concern, liquidity and covenant headroom.`],
 think:`Thomas Cook's problem wasn't its cash flow over a whole year. What was it?`,
 reveal:[`The low point. A seasonal business can be fine across a year and still run out of cash in its worst month. Its funding had no room left for that month, and it went looking for more when lenders were already nervous.`],
 lesson:`Know BraceWorks' lowest cash week (from the 13-week forecast) and lowest month (from the budget), and arrange committed facilities to cover them with room to spare, before the bank has reason to worry.`,
 src:[['FRC sanctions against EY (2025)','https://www.frc.org.uk/news-and-events/news/2025/04/sanctions-against-ernst-young-llp-and-richard-wilson/'],['Axios: Thomas Cook collapses (2019)','https://www.axios.com/2019/09/23/thomas-cook-collapses-travelers-reported-stranded']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 17 */
{m:'m17',s:'pay',co:'Hin Leong Trading',where:'Singapore',year:'2020',
 title:'The same oil, pledged and sold more than once',
 stat:{n:800,f:'$#m',l:'of trading losses hidden for about a decade'},
 body:[`Hin Leong was one of Asia's biggest oil traders. More than 20 banks financed its trade, lending against cargoes, letters of credit and receivables on the strength of the paperwork.`,
  `In April 2020, as oil prices collapsed, the truth came out. Founder Lim Oon Kuin admitted in a court filing that he had instructed the finance department to prepare accounts without showing about $800 million of futures losses. The audited accounts to October 2019 had shown a $78 million profit and $1.28 billion of stock; by April 2020 there was $141 million, because cargo pledged to banks had been sold. Court-appointed managers found forged documents "on a massive scale" and the same inventory sold to several parties. The company owed about $4 billion.`,
  `In 2024 Lim was convicted of cheating HSBC with forged documents for two sales that never happened, and sentenced to 17½ years; on appeal in March 2026 the sentence was cut to 13½ years.`],
 think:`The banks held documents for every loan. Why weren't they protected?`,
 reveal:[`They lent against paper, not goods. No one checked that the cargo existed, or that it hadn't already been pledged to another bank. Once the documents were false, the security behind the loans was worthless.`],
 lesson:`Trade finance is only as good as the documents and the goods behind them. Keep stock and receivables records that reconcile, never let the same invoice or stock be financed twice, and expect your banks to check, because after cases like this they do.`,
 src:[['Singapore Police Force: sentencing (2024)','https://www.police.gov.sg/media-hub/news/2024/20241118_founder_of_hin_leong_trading_sentenced_and_jailed_for_cheating_and_instigating_forgery'],['S&P Global: the collapse of Hin Leong','https://www.spglobal.com/commodity-insights/en/news-research/blog/crude-oil/092420-after-hin-leong-collapse-of-a-singaporean-oil-prodigy']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 18 */
{m:'m18',s:'fraud',co:'City of Dixon',where:'Illinois, United States',year:'1990–2012',
 title:'The comptroller, the horses and the holiday',
 stat:{n:53.7,d:1,f:'$#m',l:'taken from a small town over 22 years'},
 body:[`Rita Crundwell was comptroller of Dixon, Illinois, a town of about 15,700 people with a yearly budget of $8 to $9 million. In 1990 she opened a bank account in the city's name that only she controlled. For 22 years she moved city money into it, covered the transfers with 159 fake state invoices, and collected the city's post herself so nobody else saw the statements. In 2008 alone she took $5.8 million, while the council was told money was tight.`,
  `She spent it on a champion quarter-horse business. In 2011 she took several months' leave. The colleague covering for her asked the bank for all of the city's statements, and found the account.`,
  `Crundwell pleaded guilty to taking $53.7 million and was sentenced to 19 years and 7 months in prison. Her horses and other assets sold for more than $12 million, and the city recovered $40 million from its auditors and its bank.`],
 think:`What one control would have caught this in the first month?`,
 reveal:[`Someone other than the person paying the bills receiving the bank statements and checking the reconciliation. Crundwell opened accounts, paid invoices, collected the post and reconciled the bank. For 22 years nobody looked over her shoulder.`],
 lesson:`Split the duties: whoever records payments shouldn't also approve them or reconcile the bank. Have statements come to you or an owner directly, and make sure finance staff take real holidays while someone else does their work.`,
 src:[['US Department of Justice: sentencing (2013)','https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndil/pr/former-dixon-comptroller-rita-crundwell-sentenced-nearly-20-years-federal-prison-537'],['ABA Journal: $40 million settlement','https://www.abajournal.com/news/article/bank_and_accountants_to_pay_40m_to_settle_municipal_embezzlement_case']]},
{m:'m18',s:'audit',co:'Wirecard',where:'Germany and the Philippines',year:'2020',
 title:'€1.9 billion in Manila that never arrived',
 stat:{n:1.9,d:1,f:'€#bn',l:'supposedly held in trust accounts at two Philippine banks'},
 body:[`Wirecard was a German payments company that joined the DAX, Germany's top share index, in 2018 and was once valued at more than €24 billion. Its accounts showed €1.9 billion, about a quarter of its balance sheet, held in trust accounts at two Philippine banks, BDO and BPI.`,
  `In June 2020 its auditor, EY, refused to sign the 2019 accounts because it couldn't confirm the money. Both banks said Wirecard was not their client and that the documents were spurious. The governor of the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas said none of the money had entered the Philippine financial system. Within days Wirecard said the cash probably didn't exist, and on 25 June it filed for insolvency.`,
  `Investigators later criticised the auditor for relying on documents that came through the company and third parties rather than confirmations obtained directly from the banks. As of October 2026 the former chief executive was still on trial in Munich, where proceedings began in 2022, and denied the charges; another former executive is a fugitive.`],
 think:`EY audited Wirecard for a decade. What should it have done differently?`,
 reveal:[`Asked the banks directly. A balance supported by documents that pass through the company, or through a trustee it chose, is only as reliable as the people passing them on.`],
 lesson:`Your auditor should send bank confirmations straight to your banks and get the replies straight back. And you should see your own balances in each bank's own portal, not only in reports prepared for you.`,
 src:[['Al Jazeera: Wirecard says missing funds most likely don\'t exist (2020)','https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/6/22/what-2-1bn-wirecard-says-missing-funds-most-likely-do-not-exist'],['Philstar: BSP on Wirecard funds (2020)','https://www.philstar.com/business/2020/06/21/2022481/business']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 19 */
{m:'m19',s:'cross',co:'Vodafone',where:'India',year:'2007–2021',
 title:'The buyer asked to pay the seller\'s tax',
 stat:{n:11000,f:'₹# crore',l:'withholding tax India demanded in 2010, about $2 billion'},
 body:[`In 2007 Vodafone paid about $11 billion for control of Hutchison's Indian mobile business. The deal was done offshore: a Dutch Vodafone company bought a Cayman Islands company from Hong Kong-based Hutchison. Nobody withheld any Indian tax.`,
  `India's tax department said the deal was really the sale of an Indian business, and that under its withholding rules Vodafone, as the payer, should have deducted tax from the price. It demanded about ₹11,000 crore, roughly $2 billion, from Vodafone.`,
  `Vodafone won in India's Supreme Court in January 2012. Months later Parliament changed the law retrospectively, back to before the deal, and the demand returned, growing with interest to about ₹22,100 crore. In 2020 an international tribunal ruled unanimously for Vodafone, including the arbitrator India had appointed, and in 2021 India withdrew its retrospective demands. The dispute had lasted about 14 years.`],
 think:`Why was Vodafone, the buyer, chased for tax on the seller's gain?`,
 reveal:[`Withholding rules make the payer responsible for collecting tax on certain cross-border payments. Fail to withhold when you should, and the tax authority can come to you for the whole amount.`],
 lesson:`Before paying a foreign agent, consultant, licensor or lender, ask whether tax must be withheld and at what treaty rate. In the Philippines, as in most countries, the payer is liable if it gets this wrong.`,
 src:[['Supreme Court of India judgment (2012)','https://legalauthority.in/judgement/vodafone-international-holdings-b-v-vs-union-of-india-7507'],['Onmanorama: India scraps retrospective tax (2021)','https://www.onmanorama.com/news/business/2021/08/06/india-govt-scraps-retro-tax-cairn-vodafone.html']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 20 */
{m:'m20',s:'sources',co:'Ford',where:'United States',year:'2006–2012',
 title:'The company that pledged its logo, and survived',
 stat:{n:23.5,d:1,f:'$#bn',l:'of financing arranged in 2006, while banks were still willing to lend'},
 tone:'good',
 body:[`In 2006 Ford was losing money and expected more losses. Its finance team, led by the chief financial officer, began arranging about $23.5 billion of financing months before Alan Mulally arrived as chief executive, and completed it in December 2006, while lenders were still willing. To get it, Ford pledged substantially all of its US car-making assets, shares in its foreign businesses, its headquarters, and even the blue oval logo.`,
  `Two years later the financial crisis hit and Ford lost $14.6 billion in 2008. But it had the cash. In December 2008 Mulally told Congress that Ford did not need a government bridge loan, and in January 2009 Ford drew its credit line in full. GM and Chrysler, which needed money when nobody would lend, went through government-backed bankruptcies in 2009. Ford did neither, though it did take a separate $5.9 billion government loan to retool its plants.`,
  `When Ford regained an investment-grade credit rating in 2012, it got its logo back. Bill Ford called it "one of the best days that I can remember".`],
 think:`Ford borrowed heavily when it didn't yet need the money. Why was that the right call?`,
 reveal:[`Lenders lend most readily when you need it least. In a crisis, borrowing capacity disappears exactly when you need it. Ford bought its headroom while it was still for sale.`],
 lesson:`Arrange BraceWorks' facilities while results are good: a working capital line sized for your worst plausible quarter, and the equipment loan before you order the machine. Headroom is cheapest to buy before you need it.`,
 src:[['Simpson Thacher: Ford $23.5 billion financing (2006)','https://www.stblaw.com/about-us/news/view/2006/12/19/simpson-thacher-represents-ford-motor-company-in-$23-5-billion-financing-'],['Gardner Business Media: Ford reclaims the blue oval (2012)','https://www.gardnerweb.com/news/ford-reclaims-rights-to-blue-oval']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 21 (judgement calls) */
{m:'m24',s:'revenue',co:'Sunbeam',where:'United States',year:'1997–1998',
 title:'The barbecue grills that all came back',
 stat:{n:62,f:'$#m',l:'of 1997 income the SEC found to be fraudulent'},
 body:[`Sunbeam, the US maker of appliances and barbecue grills, hired Al "Chainsaw Al" Dunlap in 1996 to turn it round. By 1997 its results looked transformed. Behind them, the US SEC later found, were "bill-and-hold" sales: customers were invoiced for goods that stayed in Sunbeam's warehouses, with Sunbeam paying for storage and insurance and often agreeing to take the goods back. The SEC called them "little more than projected orders disguised as sales".`,
  `In one example, in March 1997 Sunbeam booked $1.5 million of revenue on barbecue grills "sold" to a wholesaler that was free to return them. Every one came back within six months. Profits had also been stored in "cookie jar" reserves at the end of 1996 and released in 1997.`,
  `At least $62 million of the $189 million of income Sunbeam reported for 1997 was fraudulent. After the board fired Dunlap in 1998, the restatement cut 1997 net income from $109 million to $38 million. Sunbeam went bankrupt in 2001. Dunlap paid a $500,000 penalty and was barred for life from running a public company, and the auditor paid $110 million to settle shareholders' claims.`],
 think:`Which part of the bill-and-hold test did these sales fail?`,
 reveal:[`The risk of owning the goods never passed. Sunbeam kept paying for storage and insurance, and customers could send the goods back, so control hadn't transferred. The invoices were real; the sales weren't.`],
 lesson:`At quarter-end, look for orders with unusual terms: storage at your expense, rights of return, extended payment, or a distributor taking far more than it sells on. Revenue counts when the customer takes control, not when the invoice is printed.`,
 src:[['SEC administrative proceeding against Sunbeam (2001)','https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/33-7976'],['SEC litigation release: Dunlap settlement (2002)','https://www.sec.gov/litigation/litreleases/lr17710.htm']]},
{m:'m24',s:'rules',co:'General Motors',where:'United States',year:'2007',
 title:'A $39 billion loss that cost no cash',
 stat:{n:39,f:'$#bn',l:'loss in one quarter, almost all from writing off deferred tax assets'},
 body:[`In November 2007 General Motors reported a loss of about $39 billion for a single quarter: $68.85 for every share. It was one of the largest quarterly losses ever reported.`,
  `Almost all of it was one accounting entry. Over years of losses GM had built up deferred tax assets: future tax savings from past losses and other timing differences, counted as assets on the expectation that profits would return to use them. After a three-year cumulative loss, problems at its finance arm and weakening car markets, the accounting rules required GM to accept that those future profits were too uncertain, and to write the asset off.`,
  `GM's finance chief stressed that the charge had no impact on cash. But the asset had only ever been as good as the profits it relied on. In June 2009 GM filed for bankruptcy.`],
 think:`How can an asset worth tens of billions vanish overnight?`,
 reveal:[`Because a deferred tax asset is a forecast: tax you'll save if you make profits. When the evidence says the profits are unlikely, the asset is written down, and a non-cash charge can still wipe out equity.`],
 lesson:`If BraceWorks carries tax losses forward, remember they expire (in the Philippines, usually after three years) and are worth something only if profits arrive in time. Don't treat them as money in the bank.`,
 src:[['GM Q3 2007 results (SEC filing)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000040730/000095012407005630/k21364exv99w1.htm'],['GM announcement on deferred tax assets (SEC filing)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000040730/000004073007000041/dtaex99110707.txt']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 22 (real reports) */
{m:'m21',s:'notes',co:'Enron',where:'United States',year:'2001',
 title:'"How exactly does Enron make its money?"',
 stat:{n:7,f:'#%',l:'return on capital, below a cost of capital of about 9%'},
 body:[`In early 2001 Enron was one of America's most admired companies, with shares trading at about 55 times earnings. In March, Fortune published an article by Bethany McLean called "Is Enron Overpriced?". It asked a simple question: how exactly does Enron make its money?`,
  `She had read the filings. Return on invested capital was about 7%. Net debt had reached $13 billion. And operating cash flow had fallen from $1.6 billion in 1998 to about $100 million in the first nine months of 2000. The short seller Jim Chanos had started from the same place, Enron's annual report and its notes. He later told Congress that Enron's return on capital was "a paltry 7% before taxes", below a cost of capital he put at about 9%.`,
  `In October 2001 Enron cut shareholders' equity by $1.2 billion over deals with partnerships run by its own finance chief. In November it restated five years of results. On 2 December it filed for bankruptcy, then the largest in US history.`],
 think:`Most readers saw a fast-growing star. What did the two readers who spotted trouble actually do?`,
 reveal:[`They read the cash flow statement and the notes, and compared the return on capital with the cost of capital. Profits that didn't turn into cash, returns below the cost of money, and related-party footnotes nobody could explain were all in public documents.`],
 lesson:`When you read a customer's, supplier's or competitor's annual report, do what you did in the hunts: compare profit with operating cash flow, work out the return on capital, and read the notes on related parties and debt. A footnote you can't explain is a question to ask.`,
 src:[['Fortune: "Is Enron Overpriced?" (2001)','https://fortune.com/article/is-enron-overpriced-fortune-2001/'],['Jim Chanos testimony to Congress (2002)','https://hedgefundalpha.com/strategies/jim-chanos-enron-testimony/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 23 (forecast model) */
{m:'m22',s:'monthly',co:'TransAlta',where:'Canada and the United States',year:'2003',
 title:'A cut-and-paste error that cost $24 million',
 stat:{n:24,f:'$#m',l:'pre-tax cost of one spreadsheet mistake'},
 body:[`In May 2003 TransAlta, a Canadian power company, bid in a New York auction for electricity transmission contracts. Its bids were prepared in a spreadsheet. The rows were mismatched when data was cut and pasted just before the bids went in, so TransAlta bought far more contracts than it meant to, at higher prices.`,
  `The mistake cost about US$24 million before tax, 11 Canadian cents a share off the quarter's earnings. The chief executive described it as "literally a cut-and-paste error in an Excel spreadsheet". TransAlta suspended its bidding and launched a review of all its processes and controls.`],
 think:`What would have caught the error before any money was committed?`,
 reveal:[`Ordinary checks. A control total (do the bids add up to the plan?), a second person reviewing the file before use, and a sense check of the largest numbers. The error wasn't sophisticated; nobody looked.`],
 lesson:`Any spreadsheet that commits money, whether a forecast for the bank, a price list or a tender, needs checks built in: totals that must agree, a balance sheet that must balance, and a reviewer who didn't build it. That's what the mistake switch in this module's model is for.`,
 src:[['TransAlta report to the SEC (June 2003)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001144800/000113717103000189/tac6k0603.htm'],['The Register: Excel error costs firm $24m (2003)','https://www.theregister.com/2003/06/19/excel_snafu_costs_firm_24m/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 24 (going abroad) */
{m:'m23',s:'tp',co:'Medtronic',where:'United States and Puerto Rico',year:'2005–2026',
 title:'One factory, one royalty rate, $1.36 billion of tax',
 stat:{n:1.36,d:2,f:'$#bn',l:'of extra tax the US authorities sought for just two years'},
 body:[`Medtronic, the medical device maker, makes pacemakers, defibrillators and leads at a subsidiary in Puerto Rico. That subsidiary pays the US parent a royalty for using its technology, and the royalty rate decides how much of the profit is taxed in the US and how much in Puerto Rico.`,
  `Medtronic argued that the factory deserved a large share of the profit, because making life-critical devices at high volume and high quality is hard. The US tax authority argued that it was a routine manufacturer that should earn only a modest, steady margin, and sought about $1.36 billion of extra tax for 2005 and 2006.`,
  `The case has been through the US Tax Court twice (2016 and 2022, reaching different answers) and the appeals court twice (2018 and 2025, both sending it back). In March 2026 the two sides told the court they were exploring a settlement: two tax years, and more than a decade of litigation.`],
 think:`Why can a price between two companies you own be worth more than a billion dollars?`,
 reveal:[`Because it decides where the profit is taxed. Each tax authority wants the price to match what independent companies would agree, and each may see the factory's role differently. The rate depends on what the factory actually does, risks and owns.`],
 lesson:`If BraceWorks opens a factory or sales company abroad, decide and document its role (routine contract maker or full manufacturer), set intercompany prices that match that role with benchmarks, and do it before the first invoice. Fixing it after a tax audit costs far more.`,
 src:[['KPMG: Eighth Circuit decision (2025)','https://kpmg.com/us/en/taxnewsflash/news/2025/09/eighth-circuit-rejects-unspecified-transfer-pricing-method.html'],['Bloomberg Tax: settlement talks (2026)','https://news.bloombergtax.com/daily-tax-report/medtronic-irs-exploring-end-to-lengthy-transfer-pricing-feud']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Capstone */
{m:'cap',s:'detect',co:'Olympus',where:'Japan',year:'2011',
 title:'The chief executive who asked about the fees',
 stat:{n:687,f:'$#m',l:'paid to advisers on one acquisition, about a third of the price'},
 body:[`In 2011 Michael Woodford, a Briton, became president and then chief executive of Olympus. Questions reached him about deals done before his time. Olympus had paid advisers $687 million on its 2008 purchase of the UK company Gyrus, about a third of the price; normal fees, he noted, are 1–2%. It had also paid $773 million for three small Japanese companies and written most of that off within months.`,
  `Woodford asked the board for answers and commissioned PwC to review the payments. On 14 October 2011, two weeks after becoming chief executive, he was fired; within days the shares had lost about 44%.`,
  `Within a month Olympus admitted the truth. For about 20 years it had hidden investment losses in off-the-books funds ("tobashi"), and the inflated fees and prices had been used to pay them off. An independent panel found ¥117.7 billion of hidden losses, about ¥135 billion ($1.7 billion) including costs, and described management as "rotten to the core". Three former executives received suspended prison sentences and the company was fined ¥700 million.`],
 think:`Woodford had no inside information. What did he notice?`,
 reveal:[`Payments far outside normal ranges: advisers paid a third of a deal's value, and businesses bought for hundreds of millions and written off within months. When a number is many times the norm, there is always a reason, and someone should find out what it is.`],
 lesson:`In the detective round, and in your own business, look for the number that doesn't fit: a fee, a price, a write-off or a supplier far from the norm. Ask about it, follow the money, and protect the person who raises it.`,
 src:[['Channel 4 News: Olympus admits paying $687m to advisers','https://www.channel4.com/news/olympus-admits-it-paid-deal-advisers-687m'],['CNN Money: Olympus executives convicted (2013)','https://money.cnn.com/2013/07/03/news/companies/olympus-executives-fraud']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 25 (group finance) */
{m:'m25',s:'systems',co:'Hershey',where:'United States',year:'1999',
 title:'The ERP that went live before Halloween',
 stat:{n:100,f:'$#m',l:'of Halloween orders Hershey couldn\'t deliver'},
 body:[`In the late 1990s Hershey, America's best-known chocolate maker, spent about $112 million replacing its order, warehouse, planning and finance systems with new software from three suppliers. The project was reportedly squeezed to finish before the year 2000 date change, and the second phase went live on 5 July 1999, just as retailers were placing their Halloween orders, the busiest time of Hershey's year.`,
  `Orders went in, but the system struggled to turn them into shipments. Sweets sat in warehouses while store shelves stayed empty, and an estimated $100 million of Halloween orders couldn't be delivered. In September Hershey warned that annual earnings would fall short. Third-quarter sales fell 12% and profit 19%, and inventories and receivables jumped as stock piled up and invoices went out late. Competitors took the shelf space.`],
 think:`The software worked well enough elsewhere. What made Hershey's go-live so costly?`,
 reveal:[`The timing and the scale. Every core system changed at once, at the start of peak season, with little room to fall back. A fault that would have been an inconvenience in a quiet month cost a season's sales.`],
 lesson:`For BraceWorks' ERP: go live in a quiet period, ideally in stages; clean the data; run a full rehearsal; hold a stop-or-go meeting; and keep extra stock and cash for the first months.`,
 src:[['Hershey 10-Q, third quarter 1999 (SEC)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000047111/000004711199000081/0000047111-99-000081.txt'],['Computerworld: IT woes contribute to Hershey decline','https://www.computerworld.com/article/1377294/it-woes-contribute-to-hershey-sales-profits-decline.html']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 26 (group treasury) */
{m:'m26',s:'fx',co:'CITIC Pacific',where:'Hong Kong',year:'2008',
 title:'A hedge that became a HK$15.5 billion bet',
 stat:{n:15.5,d:1,f:'HK$#bn',l:'of losses on currency contracts meant to hedge an iron ore project'},
 body:[`CITIC Pacific, a Hong Kong conglomerate, was building an iron ore mine in Australia and needed Australian dollars to pay for it. Its finance staff entered into leveraged currency contracts with no cap on losses. They paid off while the Australian dollar was strong, but committed the company to buy far more Australian dollars, at fixed rates, if it fell.`,
  `In 2008 it fell sharply. The company became aware of its exposure in early September, but a circular to shareholders on 12 September said the directors were not aware of any material adverse change. On 20 October it announced losses of about HK$15.5 billion, roughly US$2 billion, and a US$1.5 billion standby facility from its parent (later converted into shares). The shares fell 55% the next day. The finance director and financial controller resigned that day, and the chairman and managing director the following April.`,
  `Hong Kong's securities regulator later brought market misconduct proceedings over the 12 September circular. In 2017 the tribunal found no market misconduct by the company or its directors.`],
 think:`The contracts started as a hedge for a real need. Where did they cross the line?`,
 reveal:[`When they grew beyond the real exposure and became leveraged, with losses that could multiply as the market moved. At that point the company was betting on the Australian dollar, not protecting a cost. The chairman said he hadn't approved them, so the controls that should have stopped it failed too.`],
 lesson:`A board-approved hedging policy: hedge only real exposures and never more than 100% of them, plain products only, no uncapped losses, separate dealing and approval, and monthly reporting of positions and their value.`,
 src:[['SFC: proceedings against CITIC Pacific (2014)','https://mondovisione.com/media-and-resources/news/hong-kongs-securities-and-futures-commission-commences-proceedings-against-citi-2014911/'],['Market Misconduct Tribunal findings (2017)','https://mondovisione.com/media-and-resources/news/hong-kongs-securities-and-futures-commission-market-misconduct-tribunal-finds-n-2017410/']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 27 (controls at scale) */
{m:'m27',s:'board',co:'Luckin Coffee',where:'China',year:'2020',
 title:'2.2 billion yuan of coffee that was never sold',
 stat:{n:2.2,d:1,f:'RMB #bn',l:'of sales fabricated in 2019'},
 body:[`Luckin Coffee grew faster than any coffee chain in China, opening thousands of stores and listing on Nasdaq in 2019. In January 2020 an anonymous report, published by the short seller Muddy Waters, claimed that sales per store were heavily inflated. Luckin denied it.`,
  `In April 2020, after an internal investigation, Luckin admitted that about RMB 2.2 billion of 2019 sales had been fabricated, linked to its chief operating officer. The shares fell as much as 81% in a day. According to the US SEC, staff had also inflated expenses and created a fake operations database to support the invented sales. The chief executive and chief operating officer were removed, the shares were delisted from Nasdaq, Luckin agreed to a $180 million SEC penalty, and China's market regulator fined Luckin and dozens of related companies.`,
  `The real business survived. Under new management it kept growing, and by 2025 had more than 30,000 stores.`],
 think:`Growth was the story investors bought. What should have checked the numbers behind it?`,
 reveal:[`Controls independent of the people under pressure to grow: revenue reconciled to cash actually received and to outside data, internal audit and an audit committee that management couldn't steer, and a way for staff to raise concerns safely.`],
 lesson:`The faster BraceWorks grows, the more important independent checks become. Reconcile revenue to cash, make sure internal audit reports to the audit committee, and give everyone a channel to speak up.`,
 src:[['SEC: Luckin Coffee to pay $180 million penalty (2020)','https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2020-319'],['Caixin: China\'s regulator fines Luckin (2020)','https://www.caixinglobal.com/2020-09-22/chinas-market-regulator-piles-penalties-on-luckin-coffee-over-fraud-scandal-101608372.html']]},
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Module 28 (investors and exits) */
{m:'m28',s:'ipo',co:'WeWork',where:'United States',year:'2019',
 title:'From $47 billion to a cancelled listing in nine months',
 stat:{n:47,f:'$#bn',l:'private valuation in January 2019; about $8bn by October'},
 body:[`In January 2019 an investment by SoftBank valued WeWork, the office-sharing company, at about $47 billion. In August it published its listing prospectus. Investors read it closely: 2018 revenue of $1.8 billion and a net loss of $1.9 billion; shares giving the founder, Adam Neumann, 20 votes each; and related-party dealings, including a $5.9 million payment by the company to an entity controlled by Neumann for the rights to the word "We". A year earlier its bond documents had promoted a measure called "community adjusted EBITDA", which turned a large loss into a profit.`,
  `Investor reaction was so poor that the payment for "We" was reversed, Neumann stepped down as chief executive, and on 30 September 2019 WeWork withdrew the listing. In October SoftBank took control in a rescue that valued the company at about $8 billion. WeWork filed for bankruptcy in November 2023.`],
 think:`WeWork had a famous brand and fast growth. What did public investors refuse to accept?`,
 reveal:[`Metrics that didn't reconcile to real profit, governance that left one person in control, and deals between the company and its founder. A listing sells credibility as much as shares; the prospectus showed too little of it.`],
 lesson:`Before raising outside money, report measures that tie back to audited results, end or disclose related-party deals with the owners, and build a board that can say no. Investors pay for trust.`,
 src:[['WeWork listing prospectus (Form S-1, 2019)','https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1533523/000119312519220499/d781982ds1.htm'],['Al Jazeera: SoftBank rescue (2019)','https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2019/10/21/weworks-softbank-financing-could-mean-full-exit-for-adam-neumann/']]}

];
BW.stories=S;

/* ---------- the card ---------- */
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"]/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;'}[c]));
const fmtN=(n,d)=>n.toLocaleString('en-US',{minimumFractionDigits:d||0,maximumFractionDigits:d||0});
const calm=()=>{ try{ return window.matchMedia&&window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches; }catch(e){ return false; } };
function countUp(el,st){
  const fin=()=>{ el.textContent=st.f.replace('#',fmtN(st.n,st.d)); };
  if(calm()||typeof IntersectionObserver==='undefined'||typeof requestAnimationFrame==='undefined'){ fin(); return; }
  el.textContent=st.f.replace('#',fmtN(0,st.d));
  const io=new IntersectionObserver(es=>{ if(!es.some(e=>e.isIntersecting)) return; io.disconnect(); const t0=performance.now(), dur=1100;
    const step=t=>{ const p=Math.min(1,(t-t0)/dur), e=1-Math.pow(1-p,3); el.textContent=st.f.replace('#',fmtN(st.n*e,st.d)); if(p<1&&el.isConnected) requestAnimationFrame(step); else fin(); };
    requestAnimationFrame(step); },{threshold:0.4});
  io.observe(el);
}
function render(box,x){
  const fig=box.parentElement; if(fig){ fig.classList.add('story'); if(x.tone==='good') fig.classList.add('good'); }
  const host=new URL(x.src[0][1]).hostname;
  box.innerHTML=`<div class="sty-top"><span class="sty-badge">${x.tone==='good'?'It really happened · done right':'It really happened'}</span><span class="sty-meta">${esc(x.co)} · ${esc(x.where)} · ${esc(x.year)}</span></div>
    <h4 class="sty-h">${esc(x.title)}</h4>
    <div class="sty-grid"><div class="sty-stat"><b class="sty-n" aria-hidden="true"></b><span>${esc(x.stat.l)}</span><span class="sr">${esc(x.stat.f.replace('#',fmtN(x.stat.n,x.stat.d)))} ${esc(x.stat.l)}</span></div>
    <div class="sty-body prose">${x.body.map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join('')}</div></div>
    <div class="sty-think"><span class="sty-k">Pause and think</span><p>${x.think}</p><button type="button" class="btn small sty-btn" aria-expanded="false">See what went wrong</button>
      <div class="sty-reveal" hidden>${x.reveal.map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join('')}<div class="sty-lesson"><span class="sty-k">For BraceWorks, and for you</span><p>${x.lesson}</p></div></div></div>
    <p class="sty-src">Sources: ${x.src.map(s=>`<a href="${esc(s[1])}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">${esc(s[0])}</a>`).join(' · ')}</p>`;
  countUp(box.querySelector('.sty-n'),x.stat);
  const b=box.querySelector('.sty-btn'), r=box.querySelector('.sty-reveal');
  b.onclick=()=>{ const open=r.hidden; r.hidden=!open; b.setAttribute('aria-expanded',String(open)); b.textContent=open?'Hide the answer':(x.tone==='good'?'See why it worked':'See what went wrong'); };
  if(x.tone==='good') b.textContent='See why it worked';
  void host;
}

/* ---------- place each story at the end of its section (fig blocks never shift saved keys) ---------- */
S.forEach(x=>{ const m=BW.mods[x.m]||BW.packs[x.m]; const sec=m&&m.sections.find(s=>s.id===x.s); if(!sec){ console.error('stories: no section '+x.m+'.'+x.s); return; }
  sec.blocks.push({t:'fig',story:true,render:(box)=>render(box,x)}); });
})();
